what hybrid means to customer,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.630
and what are their pain points?

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.790
What are their drivers?

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.130
And what are the tools that
are available for making

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.790
their journey to hybrid
easy as well as less risky?

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:36.610
Over to you.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:36.876
ERIC HANSELMAN: Oh.

00:00:36.876 --> 00:00:37.542
Thanks, Lakshmi.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.250
Going to kick this off.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.060
Ah, technology.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.120
We're moving forward.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.220
So I wanted to touch on
the morning's keynotes.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.910
Hopefully most of you saw them.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.690
Urs was talking about
enterprises need to transform

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.200
and some of the challenges
that they're facing simply

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.980
because of market pressures,
customers' demands,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.560
a whole set of different
pieces that are really

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.059
forcing a need to transform.

00:01:06.059 --> 00:01:07.850
And what we're going
be talking about today

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.320
is really some statistics about
what's driving some of that,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.390
but also some of
the capabilities

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.580
that can help move that forward.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.560
Technology is not
standing still,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.750
which is an advantage
generally, but can also be a--

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.490
generate a little
bit of risk in terms

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.590
of concerns about what's
the right path forward

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.420
and how to really look at this.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.180
If we think about movement
to new capabilities

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.460
and infrastructure,
there's tremendous desire.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.970
Everybody wants to
get to a better place.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:42.290
Whether or not that happens
to be peaches and sunsets,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.620
new capabilities in terms
of infrastructure or cloud

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.294
developments, whatever
that happens to be,

00:01:48.294 --> 00:01:50.210
there's a lot of push
to be able to get there.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.800
I think we all as
humans want to be

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.610
able to move towards a
better place, whatever

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.440
that capability is.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.300
Exactly how they
get there and what

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.400
they're doing to make
those transitions happen,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.520
though, are really
changing very dramatically

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.920
in terms of what enterprises
are expecting today.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.380
And I wanted to
bring up some data.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.530
451 has a broad study.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.010
We have about a 60,000
participant panel

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:20.190
that are looking at
transitions in technology,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.540
budgetary intent, spending.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.998
And this is the
data that I'm going

00:02:22.998 --> 00:02:24.750
to be talking about today.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:27.030
This happens to be from
our Voice of the Enterprise

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.080
digital pulse about
transitions and on-prem data.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.550
And the thing that's--

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.210
the desire part of this
is, if you look at today,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.230
half of the respondents are
identifying that between 3/4

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.160
and all of their infrastructure
is on-prem today.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.030
And about another quarter
have about half to 3/4.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.770
So that's a really
large population

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.800
that are primarily on-prem and
probably that's not a surprise

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.187
to most of you, hopefully not.

00:02:55.187 --> 00:02:57.270
The thing I think it's
interesting from the desire

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.960
perspective is you look at where
their expectations about where

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.200
they want to get
to in two years.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.480
We're talking about a
pretty dramatic change

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.080
in terms of capabilities,
both in what's on-prem today

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.870
and what they expect
to be able to move out.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.024
Again, from an
aspirational perspective,

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:16.440
you know there's
that desire to be

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.210
able to get to
better infrastructure

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.730
to be able to manage
your resources better.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:24.760
It's a key part of what some
of those shifts are looking at.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.610
But it's a pretty
dramatic shift.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.950
In order to do this,
enterprises are really

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.050
going to need a whole
set of capabilities

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.967
that they may not have today.

00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:34.300
LAKSHMI SHARMA: And that's fair.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.990
Like an infrastructure
like what we have at Google

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.180
in the networking
space, we're talking

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.390
about migrating loads of data
in two years like the number

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:43.110
shows.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.660
So capabilities
like interconnect

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.640
that we have, the
amount of fiber

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.020
that we have put in
any part of the world.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.790
So those capabilities may be
useful for those customers,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.210
because you are migrating
workloads, migrating data,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.610
so you would need
that infrastructure

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:00.600
support to do that.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.540
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well, and as you
start to expand into new areas,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.577
it's not only just
the migration part.

00:04:05.577 --> 00:04:07.410
But it's all those paths
to data that you've

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.734
got to maintain once you're
starting to move once,

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.400
you're in the middle
of that transition,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.010
and into whatever next
state you wind up getting.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.190
So interconnection winds
up being really critical.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.140
It's something that
I think, generally,

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.600
in the move to the
early stages of cloud,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.660
enterprises really haven't
fully understood precisely how

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.580
important that is.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.669
We've all dealt with
VPN connections,

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:31.710
and then internet access
to be able to manage it,

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.760
but in order to get enterprise
capable performance,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.632
you have to have
interconnection that's

00:04:37.632 --> 00:04:39.090
going to be dependable
enough to be

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:40.580
able to manage this transition.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.210
LAKSHMI SHARMA:
That's interesting.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.317
I joined Google six months ago.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:46.400
And it's very interesting
to hear when people say,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.660
oh, network is seamless.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.365
And that's exactly you
want customers to see--

00:04:51.365 --> 00:04:55.020
it's secured, and it's
seamless, and it's just

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.260
connecting you where you
want, bringing network to you,

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.850
bringing cloud to you.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:03.780
That's what we do in
cloud infrastructure.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.880
Even with geoexpansions
and all the expansions

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.960
that we do in every
part of the world,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.730
we continue to add more fibers.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.750
We have 13 subsea
cables that we have.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.760
We have 125 PoP locations.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.620
We have loads of dedicated
interconnection and with SLA.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.130
So I believe there are
no other companies that

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.260
offer that kind of SLA
for interconnect exactly

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.310
for the reason that we believe
that if its network has

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.059
to be there, performant,
and being able to give you

00:05:32.059 --> 00:05:33.600
the guarantees that
you need in order

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.210
to migrate your workloads, in
order to migrate your data.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.050
So yep.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.190
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well,
and that seamless piece

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.564
is what's important, because
enterprise has traditionally

00:05:40.564 --> 00:05:42.720
been used to buy
capacity discretely.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.010
I'm going to buy circuits.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.090
I'm going to buy links.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.790
I'm going to buy ports,
all these pieces.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:51.530
It's being able to actually
take that capability

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.330
and bring it into an environment
that is much easier to use

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.130
and easier to consume
that helps reduce risk.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.590
The other piece that I wanted
to identify that I thought

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.170
was interesting about
this transition,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.605
if you look at what
this transition is going

00:06:06.605 --> 00:06:08.730
to look like, when you
think about how that's going

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.270
to be consumed, this is a
breakdown of expectations

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.610
of where enterprises are--

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.590
how they're expecting
to move workloads

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.400
and how they're expecting
to manage that movement.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.260
The top, the
modernized piece there,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.920
is really taking a look at--

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:26.790
easy for me to say--

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.040
expecting that they're
going to take their existing

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.490
environments, run them
on-prem, and then start

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.360
to be able to bring
modern applications.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.160
So this is mobile apps that wrap
around stuff that's on-prem.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.260
That's a good first step.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:45.570
But it's also, again, one
of these low risk first step

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.380
enterprise strategies.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.465
The second, that's a
good step down from that,

00:06:49.465 --> 00:06:51.840
though, but I think it's one
of the more critical pieces,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.140
is refactoring and
shift, being able to take

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.160
key aspects of those
business applications,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.920
refactoring what makes
sense, basically pulling

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.530
out some of those
key value functions,

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.230
and being able to take those
into the new infrastructure,

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.660
wherever that winds up being.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.490
The next beyond that
is repurchasing shift.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:14.880
That's just simply we're
going to go take capabilities

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.030
that somebody else has got and
we're going to run it that way.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.640
We're going to manage
them simply in that way.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.630
Lift and shift is one of those
pieces that, for a long time,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.500
I think was expected to be the
primary way enterprise would go

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.380
forward, we just take the
application, we pick it up,

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.840
we bring it into
cloud, colocation,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.880
whatever it happens to be.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.800
But enterprises have understood
that now that's actually--

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.090
that's a little more
complex than originally it

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:43.770
was planned to be.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:45.750
It is an area, though,
that we think about what

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.760
that transition looks like.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.360
It can make a lot of sense for
large, monolithic functional

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:52.320
elements.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.400
So if we think about
things like databases,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.830
big pieces of that
nature, those can

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.650
be places where that
functionality actually

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.170
makes sense to migrate closer
to where you expect it to be,

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:08.482
so sources of data, management
systems, or sources of truth.

00:08:08.482 --> 00:08:09.690
That can be a big transition.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.150
It can be useful there.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.090
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So that's
an interesting one.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:15.750
So let's say for
databases, do you

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.820
hear that those
databases will still

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.536
be managed by the database
providers or the IT teams,

00:08:22.536 --> 00:08:24.410
or where do you see the
manageability aspect?

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.826
ERIC HANSELMAN:
It's a transition,

00:08:25.826 --> 00:08:28.370
so there's a big piece
of the management,

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.831
of the operational management,
that I think if enterprises

00:08:30.831 --> 00:08:33.330
could hand that off to a trusted
partner who is managing it,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.860
would make a lot of sense.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.080
In some cases, they
simply want to make sure

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.450
that all of that
functionality moves over.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:43.620
But they don't want to carry
with all of the management

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:44.940
tasks that come with it.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:46.405
That can be a real challenge.

00:08:46.405 --> 00:08:48.780
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So there are
certain things we are doing,

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:53.550
and some of you if you plan
to attend keynotes tomorrow

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.670
and the networking sessions,
they will be talking about some

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.354
of the managed aspects and
how do we bring manageability

00:09:00.354 --> 00:09:01.770
and the latency
and the throughout

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:03.478
that we offer through
our infrastructure.

00:09:03.478 --> 00:09:06.660
So please, I would encourage you
to attend the keynote session

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.035
tomorrow where you will hear
a lot of things around this.

00:09:09.035 --> 00:09:10.576
ERIC HANSELMAN:
Well, especially when

00:09:10.576 --> 00:09:13.380
you get to newer
technologies like transitions

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.220
into analytics infrastructure,
things like building out

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.460
Hadoop clusters, the management
of any of these pieces

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:22.800
can be a challenge
and especially when

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.920
there are already difficulties
in making sure that you've got

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.810
sufficient staff to be
able to manage them.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.820
That can help to free
up existing staff

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.830
to be able to leverage
the high value

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.600
capabilities, the
use of the analytics,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.420
as opposed to having to spend
a lot of time adding staff

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.466
to manage the actual
operational piece.

00:09:38.466 --> 00:09:40.340
You know, enterprises
want the functionality.

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.540
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So in the
refactor and modernize,

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.030
we talk a lot about
microservices.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:51.150
And a lot of our customers
come to us for help with that.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:54.000
Many of you would
know our efforts

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.890
in Istio and
Kubernetes and the work

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.330
that we're doing in that space.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:00.060
We have a lot of sessions
coming up around that area too.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.960
So we hear a lot of customers
moving towards microservices.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:08.140
And we do take pride in the
fact that we launch billions

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:10.770
of containers every
week in Google Cloud,

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.760
like new Kubernetes
came out of Borg.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.722
And that's how we run
our infrastructure.

00:10:15.722 --> 00:10:17.430
So how do you-- what
kind of applications

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:20.490
do you see when you think
about modernizations?

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.710
And do you, from a
refactoring perspective,

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.950
do you see customers first
refactoring and trying

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.240
them on-prem and then
moving to managed,

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.970
lets say, cloud,
or do you expect

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:33.455
to see a management
across hybrid cloud?

00:10:33.455 --> 00:10:34.830
ERIC HANSELMAN:
Well, there's got

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:36.510
to be-- from an
operational perspective,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.000
you've got to have some
level of unified management

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.650
across that entire environment.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:44.160
I mean the ideal, you know
that sandy beach that people

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.910
are trying to get to, is
an equivalent capability

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.130
across all of the
different execution venues

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:53.970
that you've got and to
be able to manage that

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.630
with the same set of tools
across that environment.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.310
I think for a lot
of enterprises,

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:04.650
they expect that to be a
nirvana they just can't get to,

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.230
because they've been used
to treating the cloud

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.810
infrastructure differently
than the way in which they

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:12.420
run what they've got on-prem.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.330
And that can present
some real challenges

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:18.850
in terms of your mobility
and workload mobility.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.720
It's really hard to be able
to do that shift effectively.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.000
If you've got different
management capabilities,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.830
you've got to run and
operate differently

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.630
in terms of where
those capabilities are.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.730
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Very good.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.920
ERIC HANSELMAN: In terms
of the applications,

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.500
where we see a lot
of those directions

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.060
really is across the map.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:39.270
There's some vertical variation
that we see by industry,

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.980
but the expectation
to get towards

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:45.720
a re-architected environment
that can better take advantage

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:48.270
is a very strong piece of that.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.160
But again, that's
some variability

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:53.017
in terms of where that
all gets directed.

00:11:53.017 --> 00:11:54.600
We want to keep this
interactive and I

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:56.040
wanted a quick show of hands.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.340
In terms of the lift
and shift capabilities,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:02.130
for folks who are here from
the enterprise side of things,

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.850
how many of you are expecting
to be able to fully refactor

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.616
any of your key business apps?

00:12:08.616 --> 00:12:10.620
Quick show of hands.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:11.960
A handful here and there.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:14.570
Again, probably where are we--

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:18.670
about 18%, maybe a little less.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.890
How many of you are
expecting to be able to build

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:25.056
net new in new infrastructure?

00:12:25.056 --> 00:12:26.035
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Wow.

00:12:26.035 --> 00:12:26.910
ERIC HANSELMAN: Yeah.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:30.360
So one of the things that I tend
to see maybe a little stronger.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.430
You're here at Next.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.470
You're probably a
little more cloud aware

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.405
in terms of what your directions
are and where you're headed.

00:12:38.405 --> 00:12:39.780
Another data point
that I thought

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.540
was particularly
interesting in all of this

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:45.750
is the shift to what those
infrastructures are actually

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.250
going to look like.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.850
And we start thinking about
what the transitions--

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.872
yes, people are
getting off-prem,

00:12:52.872 --> 00:12:54.330
but where are they
actually heading

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.910
and what are those expectations?

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:58.850
Today, again, most of the
infrastructure that enterprise

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:00.480
is working with is on-prem.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.520
No big surprise there.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.430
There is a reasonable amount
of on-prem private cloud

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.970
capability today, a bit
of hosted private cloud,

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:13.710
not a lot in terms of what
the general breakdown is,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:17.750
a fair amount of use of
SaaS and co-location.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:18.570
Again, co-location.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:19.986
A lot of cases
we've got folks who

00:13:19.986 --> 00:13:23.070
are looking to move systems
into environments where they've

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:25.770
got better connectivity,
maybe transitioning out

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:27.840
of existing data
center environments.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.770
A couple of big steps there.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:32.075
And of course, a reasonable
amount of public cloud use.

00:13:32.075 --> 00:13:34.200
But again, if we look at
what that total enterprise

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:37.830
picture is, traditional,
full blown public cloud

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:41.700
for line of business operations,
real serious production

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.270
applications, is
still relatively low.

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:48.930
A sub 10% number is something
that we see pretty broadly.

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.760
My guess is, here
at Next, there's

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:54.180
probably a little more use of
direct public cloud capability

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:55.770
in whatever that capacity is.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:59.910
But the thing, though, that
I think is more important

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.310
is what the
transition looks like

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:06.360
and to be able to move towards
a doubling of utilization

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.760
of public cloud
capabilities again

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.220
is going to require
a significant amount

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.279
of transition for enterprise.

00:14:13.279 --> 00:14:14.820
And I think that's
one of the places,

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.250
to your question
about management

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.250
and the orchestration
pieces of this, that's

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.142
where there's going to need to
be a very large step in terms

00:14:23.142 --> 00:14:24.600
of how enterprise
is really working

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:25.720
with those capabilities.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.428
LAKSHMI SHARMA: This
is very interesting.

00:14:27.428 --> 00:14:30.360
So although it says that
on-prem infrastructure is

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:33.360
going to be half of
what it is today,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.390
but it's distributed across.

00:14:35.390 --> 00:14:38.430
It seems like, based
on the current results,

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:41.460
enterprises will still contain
the hierarchies and the orgs

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.877
and the rules and the
policies, and then they

00:14:43.877 --> 00:14:45.210
would be distributing it across.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.070
So something on
policy management,

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.760
like centralized policy
management keeping--

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.660
how is the security?

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:55.156
Won't the control for security
going to be more distributed?

00:14:55.156 --> 00:14:56.280
ERIC HANSELMAN: Absolutely.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:57.760
Well, I think
that's the challenge

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.420
is that, for a lot of
organizations, once they start

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:03.540
to be able to spread
into new environments,

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:06.120
it's great to have the
ability to have scaling

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.490
capacity, whatever
those drivers are,

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:10.860
to be able to get
them to those points.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.770
The challenge is to be able
to take all of the people

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.510
and processes that
have been running

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:19.980
the organization for however
long and actually transition

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.800
them into an environment in
which they can take advantage

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:24.890
of hybrid capability.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:26.520
There are a set of
different aspects

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.410
that are going to start
to come into play.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:30.420
We already see it
today with concerns

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:34.400
about data sovereignty,
what do I put where, where

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:36.180
is the data that's
going to be actually--

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:38.970
that this app is consuming
going to be delivered from?

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.310
What do I need to
do with the results?

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:42.690
Where can I run analytics?

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.280
How do I balance all that stuff?

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.920
That presents a whole
set of new challenges

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:48.930
that, for most
enterprises, they haven't

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:50.444
had to deal with before.

00:15:50.444 --> 00:15:51.610
LAKSHMI SHARMA: So this is--

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.790
here I will kind of bring
in some of the tools

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.310
that we are offering, and
there are two talks around

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:57.397
this today.

00:15:57.397 --> 00:15:58.980
So we had been hearing
similar things,

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.188
that, hey, we have these
identity management systems,

00:16:01.188 --> 00:16:05.550
and we use this identityware
proxies and on-prem.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:07.604
And then we go to the cloud.

00:16:07.604 --> 00:16:09.270
Then we are using
managed services from,

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:10.620
let's say, from Google.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.800
So we are using your
GCS managed services.

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:17.514
And so we are coming
from cloud public side.

00:16:17.514 --> 00:16:19.680
We are coming from on-prem
through private services.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.540
How do we manage all
those access, right?

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:27.090
How do we integrate them into
a common, say, policy framework

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:30.570
so that we continue to use
the GCP resources, say, which

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:34.410
are in VPCs, and we can have
a mapping of the identities,

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:36.810
whether it is device
identity or IP range,

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:40.320
and we can still protect
the data exfiltration, which

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.680
may happen because of
existing roles, which we're

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:45.780
able to access the GCS buckets?

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.840
So we put in all
of that together

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.360
in something called
VPC service control.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.570
So you can bring your
identity or their proxies.

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.240
You can put your policies
right at the VPC level.

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.010
We have like host-based
firewalls and things like that.

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.280
So you combine both of them.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:06.119
And then you can bring in all
the context, like who you are,

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:09.040
where you're coming from, what
time of day you're coming from.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:13.692
So with this distributed kind
of access of the resources,

00:17:13.692 --> 00:17:15.150
tools like that
and automation like

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.069
that becomes very
important, right?

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:20.255
So those are the things
we had been hearing too.

00:17:20.255 --> 00:17:22.380
So it's kind of matched to
what you just described.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.540
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well, it
helps that to manage what

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.140
is that growing complexity.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:29.280
The reality is when
you make that shift,

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.150
you're now in
multiple environments.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:34.230
And just by nature, it's
going to be more complex.

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:36.540
What that demands is
that you have tools

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:39.420
to be able to leverage that.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.010
I got a ghost that keeps
shifting these forward here.

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:45.220
Either that, or somebody
really wants to jump ahead.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:49.180
And the enterprise
challenge is figuring out

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:52.480
how do you actually live in
that world in which you now

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:56.680
have got a whole set of more
distributed environments

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:59.080
with diverse characteristics.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:01.600
How do you manage
it in the same way?

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.310
Because some of
those big challenges

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:06.385
are being able to take
the staff you've got--

00:18:06.385 --> 00:18:09.592
you know, in transitions like
this I don't think enterprise

00:18:09.592 --> 00:18:11.050
is out there
expecting that they're

00:18:11.050 --> 00:18:13.341
going to radically increase
the amount of staff they've

00:18:13.341 --> 00:18:14.380
got to manage it.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:16.496
So they've got to
have simplifications

00:18:16.496 --> 00:18:18.495
that can help to not only
make the orchestration

00:18:18.495 --> 00:18:20.920
and management
piece a lot simpler,

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:23.910
but to reduce some of that
risk and that transition.

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:25.900
The point that you're
making about security

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:27.730
is right at the top
of this list in terms

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:29.920
of how you manage and control.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.440
Being able to integrate
identity is something

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:35.370
that most organizations have
had a hard time with to date.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.620
I think that's one of the more
powerful tools and capabilities

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:41.860
to have at your disposal,
to be able to transform

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:44.650
what was, you know, a
lot of those functional

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:47.230
perimeter-based capabilities
that on-prem were

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:50.380
OK and manageable, but once you
start to get out into a more

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:52.074
distributed environment,
it can really

00:18:52.074 --> 00:18:54.490
create complexities that are
hard to get your hands around

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:55.990
from an operational perspective.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:57.980
LAKSHMI SHARMA: You brought up
the product name and the terms

00:18:57.980 --> 00:18:59.676
like we do call it
service parameter,

00:18:59.676 --> 00:19:01.300
where if you kind of
put in all the VPC

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:02.710
resources and [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:02.710 --> 00:19:07.610
So, yeah, and on the people
side too, I was in India, like,

00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:10.360
there was a Cloud Summit
in India a few weeks ago.

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:12.430
And the point that you
touched upon about, like,

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:14.710
will each organization
go and hire these people?

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:16.660
Like, it's not just
about data scientists.

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.510
It's about, like, just the
whole manageability, automation,

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:21.880
orchestration, and
understanding different clouds.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:24.580
Yeah, so we hear from like
financials, and retail,

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:26.770
and across the board
in this region,

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:29.590
as well as in Asia
region, that, yes,

00:19:29.590 --> 00:19:31.300
it's so difficult
to hire those people

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:33.922
and then kind of train them
on enterprise practices,

00:19:33.922 --> 00:19:35.880
while you are training--
while they are already

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.220
bringing kind of engineering.

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:39.190
But we do need to kind
of bring in a blend

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:43.120
of how organizational
structure and how

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.070
those policies and
organizational environment

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:45.940
are done.

00:19:45.940 --> 00:19:51.070
So the tools like being
able to kind of apply

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.440
education that comes
from understanding

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.900
IT infrastructure,
bringing to the cloud,

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:57.700
and applying that
back through our tools

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.997
like hierarchical policy,
like our folder projects.

00:20:00.997 --> 00:20:02.830
So we have been doing
that next, and we have

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:03.996
been hearing from customers.

00:20:03.996 --> 00:20:05.470
It's not easy to
just go and hire

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:08.350
those people who can just
do same thing across all

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:09.820
the IT organizations.

00:20:09.820 --> 00:20:12.760
So it kind of ties in to
the point that you said.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:15.760
Yes, it's not easy to just
do each of them for each IT

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:16.400
organization.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.910
That's why we're trying
to build that experience

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:21.490
of understanding
enterprise organization

00:20:21.490 --> 00:20:24.560
while we are bringing in
technology innovation.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.680
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well,
transitions are a huge help.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.965
Staffing has always been
a particular problem,

00:20:29.965 --> 00:20:31.340
but especially as
you start going

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:33.350
to new levels of
complexity, that

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:36.867
can really present some
additional challenges.

00:20:36.867 --> 00:20:38.450
One of the things I
wanted to touch on

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.630
are some of the drivers, what's
actually motivating people

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.600
to be able to make these shifts.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:47.360
An interesting point
in some of this data--

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:49.850
it used to be, the
original idea about cloud,

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:52.325
is people are going to go to
cloud because it was cheap.

00:20:52.325 --> 00:20:55.160
You know, cloud is going to
be the path to reduced cost,

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:56.600
and this is going
to be wonderful.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:00.459
Well, you know, what we've seen
over the course of the last,

00:21:00.459 --> 00:21:02.000
oh, this is a
particular study that's

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.910
been running now for a little
over five years for us,

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:09.530
is that reality is
starting to finally appear.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:11.850
And people are understanding
that while cost

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:14.630
may be a component,
cost really shouldn't

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:16.190
be the primary driver.

00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:18.446
And if you look now, this
is one of the first times

00:21:18.446 --> 00:21:19.820
in this study that
we're actually

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.100
starting to get
pretty close to parity

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:24.380
with some of the more
important and valuable drivers.

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:28.230
Cost management can
be a huge benefit.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.530
But it's one of those
things that if you're just

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.630
trying to target that
alone, you miss out

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:36.590
on so many other benefits, what
that transition can look like.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:40.280
And those next three that are
down there that are close,

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:41.780
grouped fairly closely--

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:45.770
enhancing agility, improving
access to new technology,

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:47.810
modernizing the IT
infrastructure--

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.790
are all some of those
principle drivers

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.790
that we see about the
directions of new infrastructure

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:54.210
management.

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:55.980
Let's get back there.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:02.690
The shark will appear,
but not just yet.

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:06.114
One of the big challenges in
terms of helping organizations

00:22:06.114 --> 00:22:07.614
move this forward,
one of the things

00:22:07.614 --> 00:22:10.210
that we wind up doing from
an analyst perspective,

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:11.600
is really helping
them align what

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.550
they're trying to
move towards and what

00:22:13.550 --> 00:22:15.740
those capabilities are.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.140
Agility, of course,
is a big one,

00:22:18.140 --> 00:22:21.050
being able to move
to scale rapidly,

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:23.330
being able to manage
some of those shifts

00:22:23.330 --> 00:22:26.180
in terms of managing
infrastructure with demand,

00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:29.870
being able to be a little more
flexible in terms of where

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.180
your resources are, how
your infrastructure actually

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:34.000
gets built, and what
it actually costs

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.340
you can be one of the
biggest benefits in terms

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:40.425
of moving to hybrid environments
that let you pick and choose.

00:22:40.425 --> 00:22:42.800
LAKSHMI SHARMA: And all of
this if we could do, like, you

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:45.780
know, yes, reduced IT costs
seems to be a little lower.

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:47.840
But they still hear
a customer-- they

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:51.410
do want to understand what
the TCO looks like, right?

00:22:51.410 --> 00:22:54.390
Reducing cost is one thing, but
the overall operational cost,

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:57.426
right, so if you're going
from the VM to containers,

00:22:57.426 --> 00:22:58.800
do I have to use
different tools?

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:01.140
Do I have to retrain
my organization?

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:03.920
So you talked about this
construct called VPC.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:05.750
Do I have to have a
different paradigm just

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:07.070
to go to continuous rate?

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:11.210
If you go from K8 on-prem
to GKE, or you know,

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:13.010
Google's Kubernetes
Engine, do I have

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:15.320
to understand different
things about load balancing?

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:16.690
Do I have to--

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:18.710
will the VM load
balancing be different?

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:20.940
So we do hear all of them
because, like you said,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.592
those three things about
modernizing IT infrastructure,

00:23:23.592 --> 00:23:25.550
like, how you're modernizing
the infrastructure

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:27.140
all the way from what--

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:29.210
you know, whether it will
be bare metal, or VM,

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:30.260
or container.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:32.280
And then how you will
be improving access,

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:33.560
which goes back to the--

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.860
you know, I'm used to accessing
resources through private IP.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:39.830
Are you going to tell me that
my IP address management would

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:40.884
be different, right?

00:23:40.884 --> 00:23:42.800
And will it be different
in different regions?

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:44.710
Do I have to connect different?

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:49.100
So we hear all of that, and
that becomes TCO for us.

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:51.380
Does that kind of make sense,
like operationalization

00:23:51.380 --> 00:23:52.100
and all of it?

00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:53.225
ERIC HANSELMAN: Absolutely.

00:23:53.225 --> 00:23:55.910
I mean, again, getting
back to people and process.

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:59.392
So much of what the
transition is to new models,

00:23:59.392 --> 00:24:01.100
whether or not they
happen to be a hybrid

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:04.070
or simply revamping the
capabilities that you have

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:07.370
already, are being able to
ensure that you can actually

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:09.640
bring the rest of the
organization along with you

00:24:09.640 --> 00:24:10.610
when you get there.

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:13.520
Some of that starts with
development process.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:16.340
But a big part of it has to
be the operational environment

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:18.080
that's going to support it.

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:20.824
If your dev teams are getting
way out ahead in terms

00:24:20.824 --> 00:24:23.240
of what their requirements
are, but you can't provide that

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:25.490
from an operational
perspective, that's just as much

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:27.770
of a problem as, you know,
if you can't build and ship

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:28.940
product.

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:30.734
It's a challenge.

00:24:30.734 --> 00:24:31.650
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Sorry.

00:24:31.650 --> 00:24:33.170
ERIC HANSELMAN: I was going to
say, from a dev perspective,

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:35.330
I wanted to get
another quick poll.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:38.540
How many of you would identify
that your organizations are

00:24:38.540 --> 00:24:45.790
doing a DevOps CI/CD kind of
development process today?

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:48.750
Set of hands.

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:49.770
Maybe half-ish?

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:50.930
Not quite.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:52.680
One of the things for
our numbers in terms

00:24:52.680 --> 00:24:55.290
of what we see
broad enterprise is

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:57.310
that it's still relatively low.

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:58.800
So, again, you're
all here at Next.

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:02.220
So you're probably a little
bit ahead of the curve.

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:04.470
But it's an area that,
you know, actually

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:06.600
getting the
environment to support

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.977
that transition of dev
teams can be just as much

00:25:09.977 --> 00:25:12.060
of a challenge in terms
of where this all winds up

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:14.880
moving forward and
how it can be managed.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:17.310
To your point, you've
got to be able to bring

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:19.440
the rest of the organization
along when you get

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:22.020
to that journey, so
a little bit more

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:24.624
challenging in terms
of where that fits.

00:25:24.624 --> 00:25:26.790
Now, one of the other things
I wanted to talk about,

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:29.340
time for the shark.

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.670
As well as desire, there's
also a lot of fear,

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:35.280
and we've talked a little bit
about what those concerns are,

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.430
but quite reasonably, when we
think about, especially network

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:42.780
infrastructure, there's a lot
of concern about what new models

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:46.410
can provide and
what the transition

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:50.070
risks are of being able to
move to those environments.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:51.810
But a lot like
sharks, you know, this

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:54.630
happens to be a Caribbean
reef shark that is really

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.790
good for the reef ecosystem.

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.155
They don't really bother
people all that much.

00:25:59.155 --> 00:26:00.280
But they look pretty scary.

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:01.740
They're still a shark.

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:03.870
And they are a
reasonably good metaphor

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:05.430
for what a lot of
these transitions

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:08.400
are because, I think,
a lot of organizations

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:12.180
look at what that transition
is, and all they see is change.

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:15.810
And change when it's
not well-defined,

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.880
when you don't understand
what's going to get you

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:21.600
to that new environment, can
be pretty scary for good reason

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:24.352
because you don't know what that
outcome is going to look like.

00:26:24.352 --> 00:26:26.310
And there are more than
enough cautionary tales

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:27.240
that are out there.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:31.460
People have seen things go
wrong when whatever happened.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.360
I make the point
that a lot of this

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:36.542
might be tied to not being fully
aware of enough of the network

00:26:36.542 --> 00:26:38.000
and interconnect
pieces that you've

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.480
got to have to be able to bring
that together, but leave it up

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:42.162
to [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:42.162 --> 00:26:44.370
LAKSHMI SHARMA: I would say
that it is because people

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:45.590
don't really understand.

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:48.951
I mean, they understand the
current fate of designs.

00:26:48.951 --> 00:26:51.450
They understand what have been
told to them, like, you know,

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:55.110
going from boxes on-prem
to boxes in cloud.

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:59.040
Like, say, hey, instead of
like I'll create your firewall,

00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:02.520
but I'll keep it
like a box, right?

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:05.000
It will be still throttled
because of bandwidth.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.500
A load balancer would still
be throttled by the bandwidth.

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.729
I think that's also because
they continue to be scared

00:27:10.729 --> 00:27:11.770
because of those reasons.

00:27:11.770 --> 00:27:18.180
But if they were told that the
shark is maybe like a gigantic

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:19.695
shark and then they don't--

00:27:19.695 --> 00:27:21.719
they can't even move, it
will just stay globally

00:27:21.719 --> 00:27:23.010
and would not really touch you.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.759
It will help you, like,
what we have with,

00:27:24.759 --> 00:27:28.220
you know, we do hear a the
time because we are so simple,

00:27:28.220 --> 00:27:31.170
like, if people would have
really done the distributed

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.930
kind of SDN fabric
the we we have, like,

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:36.900
what we call Andromeda,
and Edge automation.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:39.390
It's like truly self-edifying.

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:40.585
I know, I'm kind of--

00:27:40.585 --> 00:27:42.960
I'm not looking at the shark
because I'm not afraid of it

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:44.250
because nobody--

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.550
when I came to Google
Cloud, like, it's just--

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:49.990
we just have very simple things
the way they're supposed to be.

00:27:49.990 --> 00:27:52.080
And that's why people
wouldn't be scared,

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:53.700
but we want to be
able to explain

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.800
that how simple it
is and it should--

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:57.180
this is how it should have been.

00:27:57.180 --> 00:27:59.550
Just because you have
global infrastructure,

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:02.700
just because you have
PoPs everywhere and fiber

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:05.880
everywhere, that
doesn't cause a risk,

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:07.590
and that doesn't
cost complexities

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:11.050
because the software which
is on top, it's simple.

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:14.820
It's really, you know,
logically-coordinated control

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:19.080
plane which is built to kind
of scale to the infrastructure

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:20.020
that we have.

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:23.230
So people-- it's just-- it's
so simple to understand.

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:26.344
And then that's how SDN
was supposed to be, right?

00:28:26.344 --> 00:28:28.260
Like, you have a
logically-coordinated control

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:28.760
plane.

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:30.490
You put services on top.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:32.340
So services are
also built the way

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:34.551
that application
developers are used to.

00:28:34.551 --> 00:28:37.050
That's how this company built
the infrastructure, whether it

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:39.480
is an edge, whether you
talk about data center,

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:42.120
whether you talk about
like your cloud fabric,

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:43.350
it runs at the scale.

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:46.600
So I was talking to
somebody yesterday,

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:48.360
and they were asking
about the same thing.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:49.530
Oh, you are so global.

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:50.760
Is it your infrastructure?

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.250
So people forget that
infrastructure is not just

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:56.210
hardware, and
edges, and routers.

00:28:56.210 --> 00:28:59.220
Infrastructure capability
comes from the software

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.240
that we talk about.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:01.980
That's what made cloud possible.

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:05.010
And if we would have made
that smaller in scale,

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:06.870
then there would be complexity.

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.450
We have taken all
those complexities out.

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.272
You just deploy--
click of a button.

00:29:12.272 --> 00:29:13.980
Let's say you have
global load balancing.

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.840
You just go to one
place and configure it.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:18.540
Then you go to-- if you want
to bring in your capabilities

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.120
like Cloud Armor, you
just go and configure

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:22.300
another click of a button.

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.585
Whether it is containers,
whether it is VM,

00:29:24.585 --> 00:29:28.920
it is really like single point
of configuration management

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:32.040
and distributed to the
scale of the world.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.560
And if people who
just visualize that,

00:29:34.560 --> 00:29:36.330
I think they would
not be afraid of it.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:38.455
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well, I
think one of the challenges

00:29:38.455 --> 00:29:40.020
is that, you know,
we're all humans.

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:43.830
We see that doing the same
things that we've been doing

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:47.220
is the least risky
way to go forward.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.830
And I think especially when
you start thinking about

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:53.640
our application structures
and interconnection,

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:57.570
that there's an expectation that
if we just simply take what we

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:01.350
had on-prem, put it into
new environments in exactly

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:04.380
the same way, it's just--

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:06.042
that's the safest way to do it.

00:30:06.042 --> 00:30:07.500
You know, it's kind
of like walking

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:09.610
on the beach in dress shoes.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.810
You can do that,
but you are going

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:15.540
to get sand in your shoes,
and it's really not the best

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:16.510
way to go about it.

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.810
You know, put on
flip-flops, get comfortable.

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:23.160
But for enterprises,
that first--

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.039
sort of it's that lift
and shift piece of,

00:30:25.039 --> 00:30:26.830
we're going to take
exactly what we've got.

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:29.850
We're going to reproduce it
exactly the same way it was,

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:31.890
because we know that
works, without thinking

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:33.600
about a lot of the
simplifications that

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:36.900
can actually start to pull
out some of the complexity

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:40.020
because a lot of the way in
which the application has been

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:42.670
built and a lot of
those structures

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:45.300
work really well for the
technologies that were there.

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:47.400
But the simplifications
now start

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:52.190
to allow a much easier
management, better security,

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:53.940
better interconnection,
more than anything

00:30:53.940 --> 00:30:56.040
else in new environments
can actually

00:30:56.040 --> 00:30:58.860
start to help that
transition and can reduce

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:00.787
risk in that process as well.

00:31:00.787 --> 00:31:03.120
That's, I think, one of the
things that many enterprises

00:31:03.120 --> 00:31:06.780
tend to get stuck on is that
there is an expectation that we

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:10.910
simply have to reproduce it
exactly the way we did on-prem,

00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:11.460
you know?

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:12.900
I've got a whole bunch of VLANs.

00:31:12.900 --> 00:31:15.330
I need to match those
same VLAN connections

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:16.860
to exactly what
I've got, you know,

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:20.370
wherever I happen to be
despite the complexity of being

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:23.700
able to manage all those
tie-ins and the configuration

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:25.670
complexity that comes with it.

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:29.430
It's important to realize
that maintaining complexity

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.184
is also a risk factor.

00:31:31.184 --> 00:31:32.850
And I know in a lot
of the conversations

00:31:32.850 --> 00:31:37.470
we have with our clients,
the expectation that we just

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:40.260
keep things the way they are
misses out on an opportunity

00:31:40.260 --> 00:31:44.850
to actually improve
their risk posture,

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:49.320
improve their security, reduce
errors in configuration.

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:52.740
Everybody's got good
statistics on how many outages

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:56.400
are caused simply by
manual error in some

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:57.990
of those transitions.

00:31:57.990 --> 00:31:59.760
It can be a real
challenge in terms

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:01.237
of what really needs to happen.

00:32:01.237 --> 00:32:02.820
LAKSHMI SHARMA: I've
used this analogy

00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:06.000
before, you know, when I
worked in a previous job

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:12.570
that some cloud providers
who started keeping

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:15.780
the same notion as in terms of
manageability and operationals

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:18.069
at IT, when they
moved it to the cloud,

00:32:18.069 --> 00:32:19.360
they really took the mainframe.

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:22.470
So clouds are-- not
for us, cloud providers

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.400
are the new mainframes
of the world, right?

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:26.066
So now once you're
stuck, you don't even

00:32:26.066 --> 00:32:29.430
know where your storage is and
how-- where my policies are

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:31.890
managed, what kind of
visibility do I have?

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:34.890
Do I-- can I just use my
manageability and visibility

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:35.820
tools that I have?

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:39.540
Like, where is my packets
going, flow going?

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:41.850
So all that visibility
and manageability

00:32:41.850 --> 00:32:45.090
around my information, like,
say, in this case, VPC,

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:49.140
or in this case, your
resources, is not

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:52.500
easy to offer because
it's just a new mainframe

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:54.160
for some providers, not for us.

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.160
ERIC HANSELMAN: Well, and
you bring up the point

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:59.960
about operational visibility,
which is another critical part.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:02.360
You know, the move
to virtualization

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:04.735
gave organizations a
lot more visibility

00:33:04.735 --> 00:33:06.360
in terms of what was
actually happening

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:07.410
in the infrastructure.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:09.150
The move to cloud
actually increases

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:12.030
that dramatically further.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:15.060
But most organizations
don't take advantage of it.

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:17.490
In many cases, because
they're not actually set up

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:20.760
to be able to leverage
greater visibility to be

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:24.120
able to take all that telemetry
and actually digest it, which

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:28.050
actually brings me to the next
point I wanted to identify,

00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:31.770
which is that there is a
dramatic lack of automation.

00:33:31.770 --> 00:33:35.490
If we think about what
organizations are doing today,

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:37.740
we've got to get more
automated in ways

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:39.780
that are more sophisticated.

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:41.484
But having lots of
additional telemetry

00:33:41.484 --> 00:33:43.650
that can give you information
about what's happening

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:45.400
is great.

00:33:45.400 --> 00:33:47.940
But in many cases,
we keep running

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:49.950
with the same
procedures that have

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:52.230
got strong manual dependencies.

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:55.902
And this is particularly
true in networking.

00:33:55.902 --> 00:33:57.360
And it's one of
those things that I

00:33:57.360 --> 00:34:01.920
think is, for me, one of those
big transitional opportunities

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.100
that you can actually start to
do a lot more in terms of both

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:07.740
the capabilities you have, the
awareness you have in terms

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:09.573
of the amount of telemetry
that's coming out

00:34:09.573 --> 00:34:11.520
of those environments,
and the ways

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:13.350
in which you can
put it to work to,

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:17.280
again, simplify what you're
doing day in and day out.

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:17.969
So--

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:18.900
LAKSHMI SHARMA: That's
an interesting piece.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.780
So we used to hear and like even
before I joined this company,

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:25.199
other places, hey, cloud can
only offer you L3 visibility,

00:34:25.199 --> 00:34:28.380
or it can give you, like, maybe
end-of-the-day information

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:29.590
about all your applications.

