if you think about services

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:00.380
as application running
on top of an SDN fabric,

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:04.220
that's how SDN and cloud
automation was supposed to be.

00:39:04.220 --> 00:39:07.010
So they are just kind of
adding services as and when

00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:10.700
you deploy, and you can take
those services in a managed

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:11.916
fashion as well, right?

00:39:11.916 --> 00:39:13.790
Some of the things around
Istio, for example,

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:16.100
they were meant to kind
of abstract the service

00:39:16.100 --> 00:39:18.170
plane, which sits on
top of networking plane.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:21.740
But if you do not simplify
your network control plane,

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:24.560
you won't be able to
get the scalability out

00:39:24.560 --> 00:39:25.880
of your application.

00:39:25.880 --> 00:39:29.270
So they both need to
continue to go hand-in-hand.

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:32.255
And other reasons
