There's a lot of things
around application grouping

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:48.280
as well as the security
aspects, rather.

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:52.420
And it's really managed through
the seminal abstractions

00:45:52.420 --> 00:45:54.430
and I would rather say
that infrastructure

00:45:54.430 --> 00:45:55.750
that is built over years.

00:45:55.750 --> 00:45:58.180
So one is from, how
do you access that?

00:45:58.180 --> 00:46:00.430
But at the same time, for
us, our infrastructure

00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:01.667
is designed that way too.

00:46:01.667 --> 00:46:04.000
So if you talk about things
coming from [INAUDIBLE] that

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:07.300
hit our GFE, and it
just kind of-- it

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:08.850
supports our global
load balancer,

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:11.320
which supports like
products like Cloud Armor.

00:46:11.320 --> 00:46:14.680
So we are organize-- our
infrastructure ways-- also,

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:15.770
we are designed that way.

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:19.095
And we do have software that
puts abstractions on top.

00:46:19.095 --> 00:46:20.970
And we do continue to
kind of make investment

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:23.080
through the open source world.

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:26.164
And open source supports
hybrid and multi-cloud too.

00:46:26.164 --> 00:46:27.580
ERIC HANSELMAN:
Well, and that can

00:46:27.580 --> 00:46:29.860
smooth that path for
enterprises to be

00:46:29.860 --> 00:46:32.710
able to make that
transition and to be

00:46:32.710 --> 00:46:34.690
able to get to that point.

00:46:34.690 --> 00:46:37.030
It's important to think
about not only the automation

00:46:37.030 --> 00:46:40.370
pieces, but how that actually
affects operation as a whole.

00:46:40.370 --> 00:46:43.150
And to your point,
it's simplifying

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:46.150
all those operations, being
able to go bring that together

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:48.280
with a set of tools that
enterprises can typically

00:46:48.280 --> 00:46:52.720
consume, and the ability to be
able to bring that all in ways

00:46:52.720 --> 00:46:55.270
that leveraged hybrid
infrastructure can help

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:59.290
to make that transition
as painless as possible,

00:46:59.290 --> 00:47:00.910
but more importantly,
to reduce risk

00:47:00.910 --> 00:47:02.472
in that transition
at the same time.

00:47:02.472 --> 00:47:05.055
LAKSHMI SHARMA: And hybrid does
mean that multi-cloud as well,

00:47:05.055 --> 00:47:05.555
right?

00:47:05.555 --> 00:47:08.365
So we can help customers go
from one cloud to another.

00:47:08.365 --> 00:47:09.070
ERIC HANSELMAN:
Yeah, as analysts,

00:47:09.070 --> 00:47:11.240
we've got very specific
definitions for some things,

00:47:11.240 --> 00:47:14.230
but the reality is hybrid
environments are simply

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:18.070
a matter of multiple clouds,
multiple capabilities,

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:20.964
but the important
thing is to understand

00:47:20.964 --> 00:47:22.630
how you're going to
run that environment

00:47:22.630 --> 00:47:24.490
and operationalize it.

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:25.970
The points that
you're identifying

00:47:25.970 --> 00:47:29.770
are big pieces of making
that transition simple

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.630
from an enterprise perspective.

00:47:32.630 --> 00:47:36.140
And I think you were
mentioning that this afternoon,

00:47:36.140 --> 00:47:38.717
there's also going to
be a little bit of--

00:47:38.717 --> 00:47:39.550
LAKSHMI SHARMA: Yes.

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:40.760
ERIC HANSELMAN:
--some more surprises

00:47:40.760 --> 00:47:42.380
in the networking
track later on today.

00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:43.671
LAKSHMI SHARMA: That's correct.

00:47:43.671 --> 00:47:46.000
So there's a session
this afternoon

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:50.020
coming up on how there is--

00:47:50.020 --> 00:47:51.880
how we are defining
some constructs

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:53.590
in networking,
where you would be

00:47:53.590 --> 00:47:56.030
able to leverage them
and get like native load

00:47:56.030 --> 00:47:57.850
balancing in a hybrid way.

00:47:57.850 --> 00:48:00.347
And when I say hybrid, to
me, hybrid is like the point

00:48:00.347 --> 00:48:01.180
that I touched upon.

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:04.270
It's multi-cloud and hybrid
across infrastructure

00:48:04.270 --> 00:48:09.700
like doing load balancing, the
highest latency, and the best

00:48:09.700 --> 00:48:13.900
bandwidth optimization for
VMs as well as for containers.

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:15.884
So there are some
very-- you know,

00:48:15.884 --> 00:48:17.425
things we are going
to launch, and we

00:48:17.425 --> 00:48:21.100
are going to talk about, to make
this very simple, operationally

00:48:21.100 --> 00:48:23.860
same, regardless of what
infrastructure and what

00:48:23.860 --> 00:48:25.840
location do you
choose and bringing

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:28.420
all the power of like what
we talked about like CDN,

00:48:28.420 --> 00:48:31.630
and, you know, TLS support
from multiple certificates,

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:33.340
and things that
we have been doing

00:48:33.340 --> 00:48:36.160
in the face of global
load balancing.

00:48:36.160 --> 00:48:40.780
We will bring all of that to
support multi-cloud and hybrid.

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:45.070
So if you plan to attend
or want to hear about that,

00:48:45.070 --> 00:48:48.610
please refer to some of
our Kubernetes sessions.

00:48:48.610 --> 00:48:51.190
And there will be-- and as well
as like end-to-end security

00:48:51.190 --> 00:48:52.090
sessions.

00:48:52.090 --> 00:48:54.530
So with that, thank you so much.

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:56.990
And thank you everybody
for attending.

00:48:56.990 --> 00:48:59.430
If you have some
questions, we will be

00:48:59.430 --> 00:49:01.220
